# 11. TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE AND SECURITY

First inspect and reuse the existing technology stack.

If the application already has a working backend, database and authentication provider, integrate with them instead of replacing them.

If the project has no backend infrastructure and is compatible with Google's ecosystem, use:

- Firebase Authentication – Google and email/password authentication.
- Cloud Firestore – Auction, user, bid and notification data.
- Firebase Storage – Gemstone images.
- Cloud Functions – Secure bid processing and server-side business logic.
- Firebase Cloud Messaging – Optional web push notifications.
- Scheduled Cloud Functions – Automatic auction state transitions and reminders.

Use the appropriate real-time subscription mechanism for live synchronization.

### Security requirements

- Enforce authentication on protected routes.
- Implement server-side role-based access control.
- Protect administrator operations.
- Validate every bid on the backend.
- Prevent users from modifying the current highest bid directly.
- Prevent unauthorized database writes.
- Enforce auction start and end times server-side.
- Handle simultaneous bids through atomic transactions or equivalent concurrency control.
- Prevent duplicate or replayed bid submissions.
- Sanitize and validate inputs.
- Protect sensitive user data.
- Keep secrets and API credentials off the frontend.
- Apply rate limiting and abuse prevention.
- Add appropriate database security rules.
- Maintain audit logs for administrative and bidding actions.

Never trust frontend-calculated prices, timestamps, permissions or auction statuses.

Do not invent payment functionality. Keep the bidding platform ready for a future payment and settlement integration without presenting an unimplemented payment flow as operational.

